From 094f55eb6331dc34e1b09e29277ff22eae6be378 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 20:57:30 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] windowing/gbm: the modes of videos stay on HDMI FRL while the
 menus need it (NV_HDMI_FRL_KEEP)

An RTX 4080 SUPER (driver 620.70.06, with an LG C5) left
the TV without a picture in 4 of 35 changes of mode from the TMDS mode
of a video back to the 3840x2160 at 120 Hz of the menus over FRL
(with 1.0 and the first builds of 1.1). Their kernel and Kodi logs, read change
by change:
- all four hangs are TMDS to FRL; 3 came from Dolby Vision, 1 from an
  SDR video at 25 Hz with nothing else in its commit, so the state that
  Dolby Vision gives back with the change of mode (the Dolby VSIF, the
  dithering, "max bpc") is not what they share; the time from the end of
  the video to the change of mode in those that hung (0.06 to 0.8 s) is
  within that of those that did not (0.1 to 5 s); three hung with HDMI
  VRR on (1.0), one with it off and the other outputs turned off (1.1);
- none of the 30 changes of mode from FRL to FRL (Kodi's start from the
  console's 3840x2160 at 60 Hz over FRL, 2560x1440 at 120 Hz over FRL,
  the same mode again for HDMI VRR or another output) hung, nor any of the
  39 from FRL to TMDS at the start of a video, though NVIDIA's driver
  shuts the head down and trains the FRL link again in all of them alike
  (nvkms-modeset.c). What only the hangs have is the change of the link
  from TMDS to FRL, made by RM in the closed part of the driver.
NVKMS takes TMDS for every mode that TMDS carries: with the menus over
FRL, each video went from FRL to TMDS and back.

nvidia-open-kernel-modules-9078 lets a client keep a head that runs over
FRL on FRL for a mode that TMDS would carry (NV_HDMI_FRL_KEEP; NVKMS
decides where: not for HDMI 3D frame packing, YCbCr 4:2:0, or a head
that is not on FRL then), at the rate its link has, without DSC, in the
same colour format and depth. Kodi now asks for it (FrlKeep.h) in each
of its mode sets while the mode of its menus, the resolution chosen in
Settings, needs FRL (its pixel clock is above the 600 MHz that TMDS
carries in RGB at 8 bits): a video and the way back to the menus then go
from FRL to FRL. Not in the mode set of the way to a TMDS mode after a
hang (FRL_MODE_CHANGE::Fallback()), until the output is over TMDS; not
while native Dolby Vision has the output (it keeps the value it found,
and gives it back); a test of a request refused with it is made again
without it. A change of it alone is no mode set, so it goes only into
commits with one. A Dolby Vision video goes out in the same RGB at 8 bpc
over the other link: the tunnel is the same pixels. The log says when
the menus need FRL and the modes of videos stay on it, and the line of
each change of mode already says the link before and after ("went over
FRL, 4 lanes at 8 Gb/s and goes over FRL, 4 lanes at 8 Gb/s now").

OffFirst() (the output off before a change from TMDS to FRL) and the way
to a TMDS mode after a hang stay for the changes of mode from TMDS to FRL
that are left: Kodi's start from a console mode over TMDS, the menus
after a fallback, another resolution chosen.

Whether the C5 shows Dolby Vision at 23.976 Hz over FRL, and whether the
hang is gone, is measured on that card. The pure part is tested on the
host (tools/regress/kodi-host/output/presfix/frl_keep_test.cpp, 11
mutants).
